<BaseLayout title="About" description="What Ariana.md covers, and how it is written.">
  <h1>About Ariana.md</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    “Ariana” is the old Greek name for the broad highland region of Afghanistan and its neighbours. This wiki
    covers that region and the wider land of Khorasan: its geography, histories, peoples, languages, genetics,
    and notable figures.
  </p>

  <div class="prose">
    <h2>How articles are written</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Sourced.</strong> Every factual claim should trace to a source listed at the bottom of the page.</li>
      <li><strong>Marked honestly.</strong> A <em>stub</em> is a short placeholder. A <em>draft</em> has been written but not checked. Only <em>published</em> articles have been reviewed against their sources.</li>
      <li><strong>Plural names.</strong> Places, peoples, and people often have several names in different languages. Articles list them under “Also known as” and say which name is used where.</li>
      <li><strong>Contested topics stay contested.</strong> Where historians, linguists, or geneticists disagree, the article says so and presents the disagreement rather than picking a side.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>On genetics</h2>
    <p>
      Genetic data can say a great deal about population movement and mixing, but it cannot by itself define an
      ethnic group, a language, or a nation. Sampling in this region is uneven, and studies are often read for
      political purposes. The genetics section aims to report what the data show, what it does not show, and how
      confident the field is.
    </p>

    <h2>Linking</h2>
    <p>
      Words in double square brackets, such as <code>[[Khorasan]]</code>, become links to the matching article.
      A link shown in red-dashed text points to an article that does not exist yet. Each article also lists
      “Linked from” in its sidebar, so you can follow connections in both directions.
    </p>

    <h2>Contributing</h2>
    <p>
      Articles are Markdown files in <code>src/content/articles/</code>. Each has a short frontmatter block with
      its title, category, aliases, and sources. Corrections and new sources are welcome.
    </p>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
